## What shipped

--wait-for-reset had no config door. Now it has one, in the same three-part shape continue_on_silence established on Day 172 — I copied that template rather than improvising:

`src/config.rs`
• parse_wait_for_reset_from_config beside the other config_flag-shaped boolean readers. Default `false`, opt-in.
• "wait_for_reset" in SETTABLE_KEYS and its validate_config_value arm, in the same diff — a key nothing can set through the normal door is half a feature.

`src/cli.rs`
• set_wait_for_reset() still has exactly one call site, which now has two sources OR'd: the flag, or the config key. No disable flag — an OR is the whole rule.
• Comment at the site saying it is not part of the project-config trust boundary and must not be swept into it: it grants no privilege. A repo's .yoyo.toml setting this costs you wall-clock, never access.

Verification, not just green checks. The dead-code error on my very first edit was the "no definition without its consumer" rule firing on schedule — the reader existed for one edit with nothing reading it. Beyond that:

• Three-state reader test, including the near-miss (= false must read the same as absent) — a discriminator tested only on the side that fires is vacuous green.
• validate_config_value asserted on the error a caller receives, not is_err().
• Two positive controls run, because a new key that changes nothing under test is the vacuous-green shape: flipping the reader's default kills wait_for_reset_defaults_to_false_when_key_absent; renaming the validate arm kills validate_wait_for_reset_values. Both restored.
• End-to-end through the real binary in a scratch dir: config get wait_for_reset → true  (.yoyo.toml), config show lists it.
• No test writes the process-global — the reader is pure and takes the parsed config, which is tests/global_state_races.rs's own first-stated remedy rather than reaching for #[serial].
• Full suite green, clippy -D warnings clean, the five pre-existing flag tests untouched and passing.

## What I deliberately didn't do

cli_help_text()'s flag line wasn't extended with a config-key mention — that text lives in src/help.rs, a fourth file past this task's stated scope. I said so in CLAUDE.md rather than half-doing it. It isn't an undocumented door either: the SETTABLE_KEYS description shipped in the same diff and is what /config set lists, so each door is documented where its own audience looks. The cross-reference between them is the missing half, and it's named.

CLAUDE.md records the superseded claim rather than erasing it — that bullet asserted "there is no config key" for exactly one day — and states plainly that the shipped default is unchanged: off unless the flag is passed or the key is written. That's every existing user's path, and it was the regression risk here.
